      <title>Libreboot Abandons Anti-AI Policy After One Month</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Libreboot-Drops-AI-Policy</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Libreboot-Drops-AI-Policy</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:23:41 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="COREBOOT" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/coreboot.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Coreboot downstream Libreboot last month enacted an AI policy to forbid contributions made using AI/LLMs. Surprisingly, already, that anti-AI policy has been dropped with AI-based submissions now being permitted. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The (anti) AI policy began last month with &lt;a href="https://codeberg.org/libreboot/lbmk/commit/56b5f36ee6d021400b969a27c7715c37653cd662"&gt;this commit&lt;/a&gt; with profanities and harsh language against AI. Over time it was &lt;a href="https://codeberg.org/libreboot/lbmk/src/commit/35972bd6abc488adf18f10b0ba48e9f5bf6bf623/AGENTS.md"&gt;refined&lt;/a&gt; with a more elaborate explanation against accepting AI generated contributions. That was three weeks ago with those latest updates by Libreboot developer Leah Rowe. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=libreboot_ai_policy" alt="Libreboot drops AI policy" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As of today, that AI policy has been dropped outright from Libreboot. Leah noted in &lt;a href="https://codeberg.org/libreboot/lbmk/commit/cbbc12b86d79fc47d1fa91e57069c93f5a5df735"&gt;the commit&lt;/a&gt; dropping the &lt;em&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/em&gt; file, that it's an uphill battle in relying on upstream code from Coreboot and co while ultimately being unenforceable with the direction of AI/LLM adoption moving forward. Leah remarked: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"delete the AI policy &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;i'm never going to enforce it. recent observations of upstream code and the way things are going tells me that i'm going to make all kinds of exceptions to it over time, and not be able to fight against the tide &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;i got too excited writing it. i believe everything in it. but it's an unenforceable policy. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;i will not promise what i cannot / will not reliably deliver. delete unreliable promise. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;i don't care if this gets me on a list. there is one thing i hate the most, and that's hypocrites. i'd rather not be one. hence the deletion."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The AI policy is now removed in the latest Libreboot Git.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GnuPG's GPGME Affected By Critical Security Bug In Turn Affecting Mail Clients, Flatpak</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GnuPG-GPGME-Critical</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GnuPG-GPGME-Critical</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:46:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="FREE SOFTWARE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/freesoftware.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Werner Koch announced a late Friday afternoon "critical security bug" affecting GnuPG's GPGME library - GnuPG Made Easy. Unfortunately this in turn affects GPGME users like most email clients, most XMPP clients, Flatpak, Samba, and other software. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This critical security bug made public today affects all existing releases of GnuPG Made Easy. A crafted very long user ID can trigger a double-free that in turn can be used to extract data from applications by sending for example signed data. It's also possible but not confirmed that this bug could be leveraged for mounting a remote code execution (RCE) attack. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wener Koch is recommending GPGME users immediately update their GPGME to today's version. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"By using an OpenPGP key containing a crafted very long user id it is possible to trigger a double free.  This double free can be used exfiltrate data from applications by sending for example signed data. Exploiting this bug to mount an Remote Code Execution attack (RCE) can't be ruled out.  Thus an immediate update is highly suggested. &lt;br /&gt;... &lt;br /&gt;The root cause for this bug are two GPGME internal functions to read the status lines and key listings from gpg.  Initially a buffer of 1024 bytes is allocated which is reallocated if more space is required.  When reallocating the pointer to the new buffer is used by the function but it is only assigned back to the context object at the end of the function.  Thus if the function returns early due to an read error or other events the context is not updated and keeps the stale pointer to the freed memory.  Eventually the context is released and the memory freed a second time.  Due to other allocations in between the common double free checks of memory allocators may not be triggered."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;All applications using existing GPGME versions whether as a shared library, DLL, or statically linked version are affected. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details via the &lt;a href="https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/info-gnu/2026-11/msg00006.html"&gt;mailing list announcement&lt;/a&gt;. GPGME 2.3.1 is the first fixed version.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Preps Linux 7.5 Change To Better Handle Multiple GPUs With Lots Of VRAM</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-DRM-8TB-mmap</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-DRM-8TB-mmap</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:41:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A change set to land for the upcoming Linux 7.5 kernel cycle expands the mmap range size for GEM/TTM buffer objects in the kernel graphics drivers from 1TB to now up to 8TB. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Increasing the limit from 1TB to 8TB was done in the name of multi-GPU setups with lots of video memory. The change was made by Intel and given the timing of this patch presumably in preparation for &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Crescent+Island"&gt;Crescent Island&lt;/a&gt;, a.k.a. the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-GPU-270-Open-Performance"&gt;Intel GPU D Series&lt;/a&gt; with up to 160GB of LPDDR5X per card. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_d270_med" alt="Intel GPU D 270" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Intel Linux GPU driver engineer Matthew Auld explained in &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261112122049.1990826-2-matthew.auld@intel.com/"&gt;the patch&lt;/a&gt; bumping the limit: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Every GEM/TTM buffer object, including imported dma-bufs, allocates a fake mmap offset from the per-device drm_vma_offset_manager. On multi-GPU systems with large VRAM, peer-to-peer dma-buf imports across devices and processes can exhaust the current 1 TiB 64-bit range and fail BO creation or PRIME import with -ENOSPC. Skipping mmap offset allocation for imported sg buffers is not straightforward without risking userspace regressions, as existing userspace (such as Intel compute-runtime) still &lt;br /&gt;queries mmap_offset on imported host dma-bufs even when mapping the dma-buf fd directly. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Increase DRM_FILE_PAGE_OFFSET_SIZE on 64-bit kernels so the fake offset range spans up to 8 TiB (SZ_8T). This stays strictly below panthor's DRM_PANTHOR_USER_MMIO_OFFSET_32BIT (1ULL &lt;&lt; 43) carveout and within 32-bit compat mmap2's pgoff limit (16 TiB with 4K pages)."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The change for bumping the DRM_FILE_PAGE_OFFSET_SIZE limit to 8TB is coming to Linux 7.5 by way of this week's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/f507c272-be86-4cdf-a481-30e28df9dee8@linux.intel.com/"&gt;drm-misc-next pull request&lt;/a&gt; to DRM-Next.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD Contributes Integration For Content-Adaptive / Adaptive Backlight Modulation To GNOME</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-AMD-Content-Adaptive</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-AMD-Content-Adaptive</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:52:31 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Merged today to GNOME's Mutter compositor is support for the Adaptive Backlight Modulation (ABM) DRM/KMS connector property and a "content-adaptive" API. This GNOME desktop improvement was contributed by AMD as part of modern AMD Ryzen AI laptops supporting Adaptive Backlight Modulation. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Adaptive Backlight Modulation / Content Adaptive Backlight Control is a power-saving display feature for dynamically lowering the display panel's backlight brightness level based upon what is on the screen, such as for dark or low luminance scenes. This in turn can help reduce power consumption for laptops especially on battery life but does come with some impact to the color accuracy. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=framework-16-ryzen-ai-300-series&amp;image=framework_16_14_med" alt="Framework laptop powered by AMD Ryzen AI running Ubuntu Linux with GNOME." /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The AMDGPU Linux driver for a while now has exposed the Adaptive Backlight Modulation functionality and the connector property for capable eDP panels. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"AMD eDP panels expose an "adaptive backlight modulation" connector property: the panel dims its backlight and compensates in the image, trading color accuracy for power. This series only adds the compositor side: a content-adaptive level on MetaBacklight, applied to KMS on the next frame. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Mutter does not choose a level. The shell is expected to call the C API in-process; there is no D-Bus interface for it. The level is an index in 0..max and defaults to 0 (off). When the connector supports ABM, the first frame writes it, which takes the property over from the driver's sysfs interface. Nothing hands it back on shutdown; that is left to a generic DRM reset."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about this AMD contributed work to GNOME for enhancing the power savings experience with modern laptops can see &lt;a href="https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/5192"&gt;the Mutter merge request&lt;/a&gt; for all the details.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>System76 Thelio Prime: A Petite But Performant AMD-Powered Linux PC</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/system76-thelio-prime</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/system76-thelio-prime</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:38:02 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_1_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;After &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/System76-Thelio-Prime-Mini-PC"&gt;teasing the new System76 Thelio Prime&lt;/a&gt; last month, this week officially launched their redesigned Thelio Prime mini desktop PC. This AMD Ryzen 9000 series powered creation is quite small with an internal volume of just 9.78 liters but quite capable with up to the Ryzen 9 9900X being supported, AMD or NVIDIA discrete graphics options, up to 96GB of RAM, and up to 16TB of NVMe storage. I have been testing out the new Thelio Prime for the past few days so far and it's been stacking up as well as one of their best compact PCs yet.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_2_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime box/packaging" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The new System76 Thelio Prime is around half the size of their original Thelio Prime PC but doesn't compromise on performance or quality. The new Thelio Prime chassis continues to be manufactured by themselves at their factory in Colorado and is made entirely of aluminum and stainless steel and a smoked tempered glass front panel - no plastic pieces involved! It follows a similar design to the previously reviewed &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/thelio-mira-r4-n4"&gt;Thelio Mira&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/system76-thelio-major-9980x"&gt;Thelio Major&lt;/a&gt; PC redesigns that launched earlier in the year. While some may miss the wood aesthetics from the original Thelio models, the build quality remains top notch for these USA manufactured enclosures.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_3_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime panel" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Thelio Prime can work with horizontal or vertical orientations.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_4_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_4_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime front view" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The new Thelio Prime can be equipped with up to the Ryzen 9 9900X -- including the Zen 5 Ryzen X3D models, choice of Radeon RX 9060 XT / GeForce RTX 5060 Ti / GeForce RTX 5070 graphics card options, up to 96GB of DDR5 memory, and up to 16TB of NVMe SSD storage.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_5_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_5_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime rear view" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Like with System76's other in-house manufactured hardware, the new Thelio Prime chassis remains an open-source hardware design.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_7_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_7_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime graphics card cooling" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For not compromising on performance/thermals with this small PC, System76 takes an interesting approach with the graphics card and CPU/motherboard area having their own dedicated and separate air intakes on each side of the chassis. The graphics card is ultimately connected behind the motherboard using a PCIe extension cable.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_8_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_8_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime CPU cooling side" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Similar to the redesigned Thelio Major and Thelio Mira systems reviewed earlier this year, the new Thelio Prime makes use of magnets for quick-access side panels.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_6_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_6_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime angled view" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This is quite a unique small form factor Linux PC with significant design considerations to not compromise on thermals and performance. And thus an interesting target for testing at Phoronix.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_10_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_10_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime with Pop!_OS 26.04" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The System76 Thelio Prime review unit tested at Phoronix was equipped with the AMD Ryzen 7 9800X3D, 2 x 16GB DDR5-5600 memory, 1TB Samsung 9100 Pro NVMe SSD, and a NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Ti 16GB graphics card.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/result.svgz" alt="System76 Thelio Prime" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Shipping with the Thelio Prime is also the new Pop!_OS 26.04 LTS release.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_11_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_11_med" alt="Pop!_OS 26.04" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;While I've only had a few days so far with the unit, I have done a lot of benchmarking already and it's been holding up well and proving to be well engineered for uncompromised performance out of this SFF PC.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_12_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_12_med" alt="COSMIC desktop on Pop!_OS 26.04" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://openbenchmarking.org/result/2611202-NE-SYSTEM76T33&amp;asm=1"&gt;This benchmark result page&lt;/a&gt; has more than 300 benchmarks conducted from the Thelio Prime on Pop!_OS 26.04. The 300+ tests ranged from AI to graphics/gaming, creator workloads, HPC / technical computing, and much more. A gauntlet of different workloads so check out that page if interested in specific performance results out of this Ryzen desktop.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The below metrics are the accumulated data from the 22 hours of constant benchmarking for these 300+ benchmarks.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/cpu-peak-freq-monitoring-overview-acpfm.svgz" alt="CPU Peak Freq Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Peak Freq Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When looking at the peak CPU frequency of the Ryzen 7 9800X3D in the Thelio Prime, it was averaging 4.7GHz and successfully hitting the advertised 5.2GHz boost frequency of this particular processor.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/cpu-power-consumption-monitoring-overview-acpcm.svgz" alt="CPU Power Consumption Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Power Consumption Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;On average the CPU was pulling around 86 Watts with a peak of 205 Watts.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/cpu-temp-monitoring-overview-actm.svgz" alt="CPU Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Thelio Prime was keeping the Ryzen 7 9800X3D air-cooled well enough with a 75 degree average temperature under load that is about typical for air-cooled Zen 5 desktop CPUs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/gpu-power-consumption-monitoring-overview-agpcm.svgz" alt="GPU Power Consumption Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated GPU Power Consumption Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/gpu-temp-monitoring-overview-agtm.svgz" alt="GPU Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated GPU Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;GPU workloads were only a subset of the benchmarks carried out, but in looking at the peak the GeForce RTX 5060 Ti never topped a comfortable 76 degrees. The RTX 5060 Ti in the Thelio Prime was successfully pulling up to 180 Watts, right aligned with the RTX 5060 Ti's typical board power at load.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/system76-thelio-prime/drive-temp-monitoring-overview-adtm.svgz" alt="Drive Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated Drive Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Samsung 9100 Pro NVMe SSD was also running comfortably with a 51 degree average,&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Importantly, not only was the hardware performing well and not too warm, but the Thelio Prime was also remarkably quiet. Very quiet operation even when the Ryzen 7 9800X3D and GeForce RTX 5060 Ti were under full load.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Out of my testing thus far the new Thelio Prime is working out very well as a petite but powerful Linux SFF desktop PC. I will have some more follow-up benchmarks/comparisons with the Thelio Prime over the next few weeks on Phoronix.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_9_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-prime&amp;image=thelio_prime_9_med" alt="System76 Thelio Prime running" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Pricing on the Thelio Prime starts out at $1599 USD for the base model with the Ryzen 5 9600X, integrated graphics, 16GB of RAM, and 500GB NVme SSD. The review configuration as tested with the Ryzen 7 9800X3D, 32GB of DDR5 RAM, 1TB NVMe SSD, and RTX 5060 Ti graphics put it at around $3632 USD with today's component prices, Those wishing to learn more about the Thelio Prime can do so at &lt;a href="https://system76.com/desktops/thelio-prime"&gt;System76.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Fedora Incident Response Policy Drafted For Improving Workflow &amp; Security Disclosures</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Fedora-Incident-Response-Policy</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Fedora-Incident-Response-Policy</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:47:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="FEDORA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/fedora.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Fedora Security Special Interest Group (SIG) drafted an Incident Response Policy for the Fedora project. This stems from shortcomings in the Fedora project brought to light during &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/XZ-CVE-2024-3094"&gt;the XZ Utils security fiasco&lt;/a&gt; in their communications. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The drafted Fedora Incident Response Policy us a best-effort process that outlines what qualifies as an incident, how to declare an incident, how embargoed security information is handled, and related communication steps. This is a best-effort policy given Fedora is largely driven by volunteers. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Forming this Fedora Incident Response Policy is also being done to the EU Cyber Resilience Act. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about the Fedora Incident Response Policy can find the draft via &lt;a href="https://forge.fedoraproject.org/security/docs/pulls/26/files"&gt;this pull request&lt;/a&gt;. The Fedora Security SIG is requesting feedback on the policy through next week.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMDXDNA Driver Reaches Version 1.0, Ryzen AI NPU10 &amp; NPU11 Coming To Linux 7.5</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDXDNA-1.0-Linux-Driver</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDXDNA-1.0-Linux-Driver</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:16:40 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; With the upcoming Linux 7.5 kernel, the AMDXDNA accelerator driver for supporting the Ryzen AI NPUs is declaring version 1.0. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Not that in-tree driver versions are particularly important, but with Linux 7.5 the AMDXDNA driver is identifying as version 1.0. The &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/AMDXDNA"&gt;AMDXDNA&lt;/a&gt; kernel driver has come a long way over the past two years after the Ryzen AI NPU support had a late start compared to the Microsoft Windows support.  In fact, it's really only been since earlier this year that &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Ryzen-AI-NPUs-Linux-LLMs"&gt;Ryzen AI NPUs on Linux became useful&lt;/a&gt; for running large language models thanks to work on FastFlowLM and the Lemonade local AI server. Thanks to that user-space software work, &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Lemonade-11.0"&gt;Ryzen AI NPUs are function under Linux&lt;/a&gt; and just yesterday also marked &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Unsloth-Desktop-Ryzen-AI-NPU"&gt;Unsloth Desktop now supporting Ryzen AI NPUs&lt;/a&gt; including with the Linux build. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As for only now declaring version 1.0, with the Linux 7.5 kernel coming out in Q1'2027, it's aligned as the first "official release with AIE4 device support". The AMD AI Engine 4 "AIE4" is believed to be the IP to be used by the upcoming Zen 6 based Ryzen AI client SoCs. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=amdxdna_10" alt="AMDXDNA 1.0" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;So now the leap from AMDXDNA v0.10 to AMDXDNA v1.0. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The AMDXDNA driver for Linux 7.5 is also adding support for NPU10 and NPU11 devices. These NPU10 and NPU11 devices are NPU3-based revisions 0x14 and 0x15. It's not clear what products will feature NPU10 and NPU11 but presumably some new Zen 5 SoC derivatives given they are NPU3 and not the upcoming NPU4. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;These AMDXDNA patches were sent out for queuing in DRM-Next by way of this week's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/f507c272-be86-4cdf-a481-30e28df9dee8@linux.intel.com/"&gt;drm-misc-next&lt;/a&gt; pull request. The Linux 7.5 merge window is expected to open at the end of December.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Rust Coreutils 0.13 Release Delivers More Performance, Better Compatibility</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Rust-Coreutils-0.13</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Rust-Coreutils-0.13</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:16:03 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="PROGRAMMING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/programming.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Rust Coreutils 0.13 released today as the newest feature update to this Rust-based re-implementation of GNU Coreutils that is used by Ubuntu Linux and other platforms. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With Rust Coreutils there are more performance improvements over the prior release: basenc base58 about 35x, cut +23%, date +22%, join +20%, and various other minor performance optimizations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Rust Coreutils 0.13 also spent more time working on remaining GNU Coreutils compatibility issues from their test suite. Rust Coreutils is now passing 95.26% of the GNU Test Suite, up 0.9% from the prior release.  For the test cases that are actually run, it's up to 96.7%. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=rust_coreutils_013" alt="Rust Coreutils pass rate" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Rust Coreutils 0.13 also now has safer file handling thanks to a variety of changes, continued robustness work, some Windows handling improvements, and other changes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and the full list of changes for Rust Coreutils 0.13 are available from &lt;a href="https://github.com/uutils/coreutils/releases/tag/0.13.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Vulkan 1.4.369 Introduces New AMD Extension For Improving Ray-Tracing Performance</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Vulkan-AMDX-Ray-Tracing-Struct</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Vulkan-AMDX-Ray-Tracing-Struct</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:05:08 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VULKAN" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/vulkan.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Vulkan 1.4.369 is out today with a new, currently-experimental Vulkan API extension from AMD to help further enhance ray-tracing performance with modern games. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Vulkan 1.4.369 brings several clarifications/fixes to this public API that have accumulated over the past two weeks. Most exciting though with Vulkan 1.4.369 is introducing the new VK_AMDX_multi_level_acceleration_structure extension. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The experimental AMD vendor extension VK_AMDX_multi_level_acceleration_structure provides the ability for adding up to two extra levels of instances to the acceleration structure hierarchy for Vulkan ray-tracing. The additional levels provide more control over the granularity of large world updates. This can also lead to reduced memory footprints thanks to geometry reuse. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The proposal spec documentation for this enhancement to complex ray-traced games/applications describes the current problem and the solution with this spec as: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Game developers are currently forced to limit the complexity of ray traced worlds in their games. The two main reasons for this, in order of importance, are high acceleration structure (AS) build and update times, and AS memory footprints. This is especially true for scenes with lots of animated geometry. To catch up with the complexity of rasterized scenes, changes need to be made to the acceleration structure API to address these bottlenecks. &lt;br /&gt;... &lt;br /&gt;Additional AS levels allow better control over which parts of large worlds need to be re-built or updated together, and at what granularity. The use of multi-level instancing has the ability to bring down AS build costs as e.g. a top level AS level can be built over instances of lower level structures that in turn 'hide' many bottom level structure instances inside, instead of building the top level structure over all 'unrolled' bottom level instances. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In a similar vein, currently, memory footprints are higher than necessary because of the lack of real multi-level instancing in acceleration structures. For example, almost all animated geometry, even if it can make use of rigid transformations to animate, needs to be replicated to fit a two level BVH hierarchy."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Thus two extra levels of instances in the acceleration structure (AS) hierarchy for improving Vulkan ray-tracing. For the moment though the extension is experimental, hence the "AMDX" prefix. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=amd_as" alt="AMD acceleration structure hierarchy" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the Vulkan 1.4.369 release can be found via &lt;a href="https://github.com/KhronosGroup/Vulkan-Docs/commit/33d3353757d83d6aca683120114fe74cc4eaf8fb"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Cloud Hypervisor 55 Released With AArch64 Nested Virtualization, Up To 990 ARM vCPUs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cloud-Hypervisor-55</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cloud-Hypervisor-55</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:51:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VIRTUALIZATION" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/virtualization.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Cloud Hypervisor 55.0 was released on Thursday for this security-minded, cloud-focused virtual machine monitor (VMM) written in Rust. While it started off as an Intel open-source project, it's expanded in scope over time and with more organizations contributing. With the new Cloud Hypervisor 55, a number of ARM improvements to note. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Cloud Hypervisor 55 introduces support for AArch64 nested virtualization. For ARM server CPUs with FEAT_NV2 support, nested virtualization can now be utilized on supported kernels. One exception though is snapshot/restore and live migration not yet working for this 64-bit ARM nested virtualization. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Another AArch64 improvement is the vCPU limit for AArch64 with KVM virtualization has been raised from 255 vCPUs to 990 vCPUs. This better aligns given today's increasingly high server CPU core counts. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2021&amp;image=lf_cloud_hypervisor" alt="Cloud Hypervisor logo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Cloud Hypervisor 55 also brings some security fixes and security hardening, VFIO peer-to-peer DMA support with DMA-BUF, CPU hot-plugging for AMD SEV-SNP guests on KVM, live migration improvements, and various performance optimizations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the Cloud Hypervisor 55.0 release via &lt;a href="https://github.com/cloud-hypervisor/cloud-hypervisor/releases/tag/v55.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. Most of the contributors to the Cloud Hypervisor 55 release were from Meta, Cloudflare, Google, Cyberus Tech, and Microsoft.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
